WEBVTT

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.310
HTML is the new ENGLISH.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:10.166
A few years ago, producing code — even a simple web page — took real skill.

00:00:09.916 --> 00:00:11.658
That barrier is GONE.

00:00:11.408 --> 00:00:14.428
Today anyone can open a laptop,

00:00:14.178 --> 00:00:17.527
describe to an agent what they want,

00:00:17.277 --> 00:00:22.687
and get a page with diagrams, rich formatting, colour,

00:00:22.437 --> 00:00:23.787
video, sound —

00:00:23.537 --> 00:00:27.475
a beautiful ANIMATED page they can present.

00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:30.387
English is still better at some things.

00:00:30.137 --> 00:00:35.567
But the ways we can communicate asynchronously just expanded —

00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:37.367
EXPONENTIALLY.

00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:39.639
Because now, anyone can make HTML.

00:00:39.389 --> 00:00:42.538
Here is what I see at work.

00:00:42.518 --> 00:00:47.260
We write a lot of internal doc pages. Long ones.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.589
Now, more and more, instead of sending a doc,

00:00:50.339 --> 00:00:52.219
people BUILD a page.

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:54.475
They pull data all together.

00:00:54.225 --> 00:00:57.051
They connect the dots in DIAGRAMS.

00:00:56.801 --> 00:00:58.248
They animate it.

00:00:57.998 --> 00:00:59.672
And they send you THAT.

00:00:59.422 --> 00:01:00.690
The downside is real.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:05.448
You lose version control. You lose commenting on sentences.

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:07.353
The upside: you SEE it.

00:01:07.103 --> 00:01:10.896
In a way a static page could never show you.

00:01:10.646 --> 00:01:13.856
So here is my working theory:

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:19.776
present your information in ways you could not imagine before.

00:01:19.526 --> 00:01:21.800
Expand your vocabulary to CODE.
